 Multi-User Production Deployment — Brutal Analysis & Plan

 Context

 You want Turtle to be a production-level personal VA that any new user can land on with no prior context, get greeted neutrally, identify themselves once, and have Turtle remember them for the long run — without leaking another
 user's memory and without you owning their data. The current build is single-tenant ("Shriyash's personal AI assistant") with most multi-user plumbing scaffolded but not wired. This plan delivers the minimum to ship a multi-tenant
 web deployment, then layers production hardening on top.

 The final artifact of execution is a new file docs/updated_plan.md containing everything from "Brutal Analysis" through "Verification" below, plus implementing the Phase 1–7 code changes. The existing
 docs/MULTI_USER_DEPLOYMENT_PLAN.md is left untouched (kept as historical record).

 ---
 Brutal Analysis — what's actually wrong

 The previous plan undersold the load-bearing breakage. Here's the unvarnished version.

 What's already right (~70% of the work is done, mostly invisible)

 - core/identity.py:44 — IdentityManager.resolve_user(channel, channel_user_id) works. SQLite-backed, auto-creates new users on first sight. Channel adapters (apps/channels/whatsapp.py:140, slack.py:144, imessage.py) already call
 it.
 - apps/turtle_server.py:1742 — WebSocket handshake calls authenticate_websocket() which already returns a user_id (or falls back to "local_dev_user" in non-cloud mode).
 - apps/turtle_server.py:1762-1804 — PersonalMemoryStore, JournalStore, ConfirmationGate, SharedState are all constructed per-WebSocket with user_id=user_id. Not module singletons. This is the hardest part of multi-tenancy and it's
 done.
 - core/personal_memory_store.py:31 — Constructor signature is def __init__(self, user_id: str = "default", ...). Already keyed.
 - Memory event schema (journal JSONL, MarkdownMemoryDocument) is user-agnostic by design — the path segregates users, no schema columns needed.
 - core/paths.py:41 — rag_vector_dir(user_id) actually uses user_id correctly. (The one path helper that doesn't lie.)

 The four blockers (one is critical, three are mechanical)

 Blocker 1 — personal_memory_dir(user_id) is a lie. core/paths.py:28-31:
 def personal_memory_dir(user_id: str) -> Path:
     path = PERSONAL_MEMORY_DIR    # ← user_id silently dropped
     path.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
     return path
 This is the entire multi-tenancy bug condensed into 4 lines. Every per-user object above (PersonalMemoryStore, JournalStore, ConfirmationGate, PersonalMemoryPromptBuilder) calls this helper. They each think they're writing to a
 per-user dir; they're actually all writing to data/memory/personal/. Fixing this one function fixes ~90% of memory tenancy. personal_memory_file() and personal_journal_dir() delegate to it, so they're fixed transitively.

 Blocker 2 — System prompt hardcodes "Shriyash". core/system_prompts/main_assistant.txt:2: "You are Turtle, Shriyash's personal AI assistant." Every stranger gets greeted as belonging to someone else. Also
 core/system_prompts/email_agent.txt:14 hardcodes the bot email iamturtleai@gmail.com, which is fine (it's the bot, not the user), but should be moved to config so a future fork can rebrand.

 Blocker 3 — RAG vector store is shared. apps/turtle_server.py:1777,1782 instantiate TurtleRAGSystem() and FAISSVectorStore() with no user_id. rag/storage/vector_storage.py:31 defaults to rag_vector_dir("default"). Chunk metadata
 has no user_id field. New users' embedded documents land in everyone's index. This must be either (a) per-user FAISS dir, or (b) shared index with a user_id metadata filter at query time. (a) is simpler and matches the existing
 rag_vector_dir(user_id) pattern; (b) is more memory-efficient at scale. Pick (a) for now.

 Blocker 4 — Web UI has no identity. apps/auth.py:52-55 falls back to identity_manager.resolve_user("web", "local_dev_user") if no token. The frontend never sends a token. So every browser, every tab, every stranger gets mapped to
 the same usr_* ID. The cookie/auth layer simply doesn't exist client-side.

 Secondary issues (will bite later, not on day 1)

 - core/memory_extractor.py (older MemoryStore with MEMORY_PROFILE_FILE etc.) uses global paths. Currently write_enabled=False (server.py:1760) so it's dormant — but the # Deprecated globals comment in paths.py:13 admits the debt.
 Leave it disabled or remove the cold-storage instantiation.
 - No storage cap per user. A single user pasting a 50 MB document into chat could fill disk.
 - No rate limit on the WebSocket. One abusive cookie can DoS the LLM bill.
 - Confirmation gate's 14-day silence window will feel slow for first-session users — they'll set a preference, see no acknowledgment for two weeks. Need a "first-session" shorter window.
 - The usr_1407a0598825/ directory under data/memory/ is an orphan — left over from a test. Delete it during Phase 4.

 What does NOT need to change

 - Memory event schema (no user_id column needed; path is the tenancy boundary).
 - Channel adapter logic (already tenant-aware via IdentityManager).
 - Router, planner, memory_extractor content (all user-agnostic prompt-wise).
 - Session store (core/session_store.py is fine; sessions are scoped by file path which will become per-user once paths.py is fixed).

