 - core/config.py — add magic_link_jwt_ttl_minutes: int = 15, session_cookie_ttl_days: int = 90, public_base_url: str (used to build the claim link).

 Magic-link email body: plain HTML, one CTA button:

 ▎ "Hi {name}, click the link below to start chatting with Turtle. The link expires in 15 minutes. — [Start chatting →]"

 Sender: iamturtleai@gmail.com via the existing EmailTool config (settings.gmail_sender_email, settings.gmail_app_password).

 Why JWT in the link AND signed cookie for the session: different threat models. The link is one-shot, short-lived, lives in the user's email; the cookie is durable, lives in their browser. Both use settings.auth_secret_key for HMAC
  signing — no separate key needed.

 Rate-limit the /onboarding/start endpoint (per IP, e.g. 5/hour) so a hostile actor can't burn the bot's daily Gmail send quota.

 Phase 5 — Tighten the confirmation gate for first-session users (1 hr)  [DONE — 2026-05-17]

 File: core/confirmation_gate.py

 The 14-day silence window is right for a power user but wrong for a first-session user, who'll set 3-4 preferences in their first 10 minutes and see no confirmation prompts until next month. Add:
 - first_session_window_minutes: int = 5 — if the user's journal has <20 events total OR the user_id was created in the last 24 hours, drop the silence window to 5 minutes so they see prompts surface and feel the memory working.
 - After the journal crosses some threshold (20 events) or the account is >24h old, revert to the standard 14-day window.

 This is a small change but it's the difference between "the memory feels alive" and "did it even save anything?" for new users.

